# Landing Page Section System Plan - astro-content-hub

Status: APPROVED with decisions - execution in progress, phase by phase.
Reviewed by human on 2026-08-04. Decisions taken:
  - Phase 2 `logos` (used-by wall) stays OPTIONAL - product-landing `stats`/
    `testimonials` are the core, home-page `logos` deferred unless wanted.
  - Phase 3 home-page preview block: confirm scope BEFORE building (kept as
    a scoped item, not auto-included).
  - Phase 4 `maintenance` status badge: DROPPED (scope creep).
  - Unknown `sections[].type`: WARN (non-failing) in validate-hub-content,
    per decision.
  - Each phase: implement -> `npm run validate:content` + `npm run build`
    green -> local commit (no push; push is the human's step).
Last updated: 2026-08-04

This plan turns the earlier landing-page discussion into a concrete design:
landing pages are built from **"MD declares data + code registry maps
components"** — a section system where Markdown frontmatter picks section
types in order, and a code registry resolves each type to an Astro component
at build time. It also folds in the prioritized optimization items from the
discussion (social proof, content preview, CTA funnel, demo layer).

Every feature ships with **mock data** so the section renders real content
out of the box — no empty sections, no "coming soon" scaffolding.

---

## 1. Problem statement

Today there are two ways to build a product landing:

- `ProductLandingDefault.astro` renders a **fixed, hard-coded section order**
  (hero → highlights → install → features → overview → CTA). A product author
  cannot reorder, drop, or add sections without editing Astro component code.
- A hand-written override at `src/components/product-landing/<slug>.astro`
  (e.g. `vite.astro`) replaces the whole landing — all-or-nothing, no reuse
  between products.

Combined with the gaps from the discussion (no social proof, no content
preview, thin CTA funnel, inconsistent quality across product pages), the
result is: only `json-server` has a rich landing, and it is frozen in one
layout.

### Goal

A product author controls their landing **from Markdown**, choosing which
sections appear, in what order, with what data — without writing Astro
components. Component authors add **new section types** in one file + one
registry line, reusable across every product.

### Non-goals (kept explicit)

- **No MDX.** Markdown stays plain; sections are data, not component imports.
- **No new UI framework or deps.** Static output + vanilla `<script>` stays.
- **No page-builder DSL.** A section type exists only when ≥2 products
  genuinely need it; one-off layouts stay in `product-landing/<slug>.astro`.

---

## 2. Architecture: the section system

### 2.1 The MD contract (content side)

`src/content/product-info/<locale>/<slug>.md` gains an optional `sections`
frontmatter array. Each item is `{ type, data? }` where `type` names a section
kind and `data` carries that section's content.

```yaml
---
tagline: "Get a full fake REST API in seconds."
description: "A fake REST API powered by a simple JSON file."
sections:
  - type: hero              # data omitted -> falls back to existing fields
  - type: stats
    data:
      - { label: "GitHub Stars", value: "58k" }
      - { label: "npm weekly downloads", value: "1.2M" }
      - { label: "License", value: "MIT" }
  - type: features          # data omitted -> uses existing `features` field
  - type: install           # data omitted -> uses existing `install` field
  - type: testimonials
    data:
      - quote: "json-server got our prototype off the ground in an afternoon."
        author: "Ada Chen"
        role: "Frontend Engineer, Nimbus"
      - quote: "The fastest way to mock a backend for tutorials."
        author: "Marcus Lee"
        role: "Tech Writer"
  - type: faq
    data:
      - q: "Is json-server production-ready?"
        a: "It targets prototyping and demos; pair it with a real backend for production."
      - q: "Does it support filtering and pagination?"
        a: "Yes - out of the box, via query parameters."
  - type: cta               # data omitted -> uses shared productCopy
---
```

**Data-driven back-compat:** existing fields (`features`, `highlights`,
`install`, `links`) remain the *data source*; a `sections` entry that omits
`data` is filled from the matching field. Missing `sections` entirely keeps
today's fixed order exactly. **Zero migration** for `json-server.md`.

### 2.2 The code registry (code side)

Mirror the existing `src/lib/product-landing.ts` glob pattern — it already
proves eager-glob + filename-keyed lookup works in this repo.

```ts
// src/lib/landing-sections.ts
const modules = import.meta.glob('../components/landing-sections/*.astro', { eager: true });
// { hero: HeroSection, stats: StatsSection, features: FeaturesSection, ... }

export function getSectionComponent(type: string): LandingSectionComponent | undefined {
  return registry[type];
}
```

One `.astro` file per section type at `src/components/landing-sections/<type>.astro`,
all sharing one prop contract:

```ts
interface LandingSectionProps {
  type: string;            // section type, for debugging / data-attr
  data: unknown;           // section data from frontmatter, or default fields
  product: Product;
  locale: Locale;
  c: ProductCopy;          // shared labels (viewSource, documentation, ctaTitle, ...)
  docsHref: string;        // base-aware, locale-prefixed docs link
}
```

Sections are dumb presentational components: data in, markup out, reusing the
existing `global.css` classes (`.feature-grid`, `.product-highlight`,
`.cta-box`, `.hero`). New visual patterns (testimonials, FAQ) add their
styles to `global.css` under the existing token system.

### 2.3 The renderer

`ProductLandingDefault.astro` becomes the orchestrator:

```astro
{info.sections.map((s) => {
  const Comp = getSectionComponent(s.type);
  if (!Comp) return null;            // unknown type -> skip, never break the build
  const data = resolveSectionData(s, info); // merge default fields when data omitted
  return <Comp type={s.type} data={data} product={product} locale={locale} c={c} docsHref={docsHref} />;
})}
```

- **Unknown `type` → skipped, not a build error.** A future `type` added to
  MD before its component ships degrades to a no-op, keeping the build green.
- Hand-written overrides (`product-landing/<slug>.astro`) still take
  precedence over the whole renderer — the section system is the 80% path,
  overrides remain the escape hatch.
- **Validation hook:** extend `validate-hub-content.mjs` to warn (not fail)
  when a `sections[].type` has no registered component, so authors catch
  typos at review time.

---

## 3. Phased roadmap

Each phase is independently shippable, keeps `npm run build` green, and lands
with mock data. All phases: **zero new dependencies**.

### Phase 1 — Section system core (the substrate)

Goal: the "MD declares, registry maps" machinery, plus the 4 existing sections
ported to it, with zero migration.

1. **Schema** — add `sections` to `productInfoSchema` in `content.config.ts`
   (zod: `array({ type: string, data: z.any() }).optional()`); add
   `sections?: ...` to `ProductInfo` in `src/lib/content.ts`.
2. **Registry** — `src/lib/landing-sections.ts` (eager glob, type-keyed).
3. **Ported sections** — `hero`, `features`, `highlights`, `install`, `cta`
   as components under `src/components/landing-sections/`, reusing the exact
   markup from `ProductLandingDefault.astro` today. `ProductLandingDefault`
   is rewritten to call the registry. Behavior-identical output.
4. **Fallback order** — `defaultSections()` preserves today's order when
   `sections` is absent.

**Mock data:** a `sections` block in `json-server.md` (en + zh-Hans) that
declares the same sections today's default renders — proves the mechanism
without changing output.

**Verification:** `npm run validate:content` + `npm run build` green;
`/json-server/` HTML in `dist/` byte-comparable to pre-change output.

### Phase 2 — Social proof (the highest-ROI gap)

Goal: trust signals on the product landing, data-driven.

5. **`stats` section** — a labeled metrics row (GitHub stars, npm downloads,
   license, contributors). Reuses `.product-highlight` styles (rename/extend).
6. **`testimonials` section** — quote cards (quote, author, role). New
   `.testimonial-*` styles in `global.css` (token-based).
7. **`logos` section (optional)** — a "used by" name wall; reuses the
   `products` array on the **home** page too.

**Mock data:** `stats` + `testimonials` blocks in `json-server.md` (en +
zh-Hans); a `logos` block demo on the home page `home` copy if accepted.

**Verification:** both sections render on `/json-server/`; mobile check (grid
collapses); build green.

### Phase 3 — Content preview & CTA funnel

Goal: show the hub's real content on the landing, and a clearer conversion
path.

8. **`docs-preview` section** — renders the product's docs index
   (`/<slug>/docs/` listing) as a card grid on the landing. Pulls from the
   existing docs collection — no new data, no duplication.
9. **`cta` upgrade** — the section accepts `primary: { label, href }` /
   `secondary: { label, href }` overrides (falls back to today's
   viewSource/readDocs labels). Lets a product author point the CTA at
   "Get Started" instead of always GitHub.
10. **Home page preview** — a "see the hub in action" block on `/` linking
    to the richest landing (json-server) or embedding the docs preview.
    (Option — confirm scope with human before building.)

**Mock data:** `docs-preview` block in `json-server.md`; a CTA override
example (`primary: Get Started -> /json-server/docs/`) in the same file.

### Phase 4 — FAQ & completion

Goal: fill remaining trust/decision gaps, product-agnostic.

11. **`faq` section** — accordion (details/summary, no JS) or plain list.
12. **Breadcrumb on product hero** — `Home / <product>` (mirrors docs
    breadcrumb pattern) so users can navigate back without the nav.
13. **fill missing product-info** — add `product-info` for `astro` and
    `astro-content-hub` (en + zh-Hans) so every product's landing is rich and
    consistent — kills the vite/astro quality gap. Mock data by definition
    (real sample copy).

> Note: the `maintenance` status badge (original item 13) was DROPPED per
> human review — scope creep for now.

---

## 4. Mock data strategy (every feature ships demonstrated)

Rule: **no feature merges without mock data proving it.** Concretely:

- Phase 1 → `sections` in `json-server.md` (same output as today).
- Phase 2 → `stats`/`testimonials` blocks in `json-server.md`.
- Phase 3 → `docs-preview` + CTA override in `json-server.md`.
- Phase 4 → FAQ block in `json-server.md`; `maintenance: active` frontmatter;
  new product-info files for `astro` + `astro-content-hub`.

Mock content is clearly sample copy (comments in the MD or a `_mock` note)
so a rebranding author knows to replace it — consistent with the existing
"original sample copy - replace it with your own" convention in
`src/config/copy.ts`.

---

## 5. Validation & verification

- `npm run validate:content` — extend to warn on unknown `sections[].type`;
  keep existing duplicate/parity checks green.
- `npm run build` — must pass with 0 errors / 0 warnings after every phase
  (`astro check` is strict).
- `npm run check:examples` — run if `skills/` or `scripts/` change
  (validate-hub-content.mjs edits touch the skill tree).
- Spot-check `dist/` — `/json-server/` renders each new section; `zh-Hans`
  fallback still wraps the en body with the notice; `lang` + hreflang intact.
- Mock-data completeness — every section type declared in mock files has a
  component; every new section component appears in at least one mock file.

---

## 6. Open questions for the human

All resolved by the human on 2026-08-04:
1. **`logos` on the home page** — OPTIONAL / deferred. Product-landing
   `stats`/`testimonials` ship; home `logos` wall only if later wanted.
2. **Home page preview block** — keep product-landing-only for now; revisit
   scope (Phase 3 item 10) before building anything home-page-side.
3. **Breadcrumb on product hero** — confirmed worth doing (Phase 4 item 12).
4. **`maintenance` badge** — DROPPED (scope creep).
5. **Validation strictness** — WARN (non-failing) on unknown section types;
   revisit only if the section set stabilizes.

